---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'

const issues = [
  { n: 8, title: 'Build and run on macOS, report the first thing that breaks', tag: 'good first issue', note: 'No codebase knowledge needed. Currently the highest-value contribution available.' },
  { n: 9, title: 'Build and run on Linux, report the first thing that breaks', tag: 'good first issue', note: 'CI runs the suite on Ubuntu, but nothing has ever launched the app there.' },
  { n: 7, title: 'Add an engines field to package.json', tag: 'good first issue', note: 'Small and self-contained. .nvmrc says Node 20; nothing enforces it.' },
  { n: 12, title: 'Add tests for src/renderer/src/floor/vfx-art.ts', tag: 'good first issue', note: 'The effects are specified normatively, so the right answer is written down.' },
  { n: 13, title: 'Raise coverage of the panels subsystem', tag: 'help wanted', note: 'The panels coverage floor is 46.79% lines on both platforms (scripts/coverage-floors.json). The work is deciding how to fake the preload bridge.' },
  { n: 81, title: 'State one Node version floor across the docs', tag: 'good first issue', note: 'The same Node requirement is stated differently across the documentation.' },
  { n: 82, title: 'Bring the toolchain facts across the docs up to date', tag: 'good first issue', note: 'Reconcile the documented TypeScript projects, repository layout and postinstall steps.' },
  { n: 85, title: 'Update the README stopping instructions for ADR-0034', tag: 'good first issue', note: 'Explain the one-harness-per-home protection without changing the stop commands.' },
  { n: 86, title: 'Update the website roadmap to match PROGRESS', tag: 'good first issue', note: 'Bring the milestone order and the current status into line with the source-of-truth register.' }
]

const dod = [
  'Typecheck, lint and the test suite pass locally and in CI',
  'New behaviour has tests at the right level; every fixed bug has a regression test',
  'Evidence is attached to the pull request — the output, not the claim',
  'Docs updated in the same PR: design document if the design changed, a new decision record if policy demanded one',
  'Degradation paths implemented and visible, never silent',
  'No new lint-boundary violations, no schema without a version, no stray hex colours',
  'The seam is tested, or its absence is recorded with the reason'
]
---

<Base
  title="Contribute — Ephesus"
  description="Ephesus is pre-alpha and builds from source. Here is what a fresh clone actually gives you, the rules that are unusual, and {issues.length} issues you could pick up today.">

  <section class="pagehead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Help wanted</span>
      <h1>Contribute</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Ephesus is early, opinionated about how work is done, and both of those are on purpose.
        This page tells you exactly what you are walking into so you can decide whether it is
        for you.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Read this first</span>
      <h2>There is no installer, and that is the honest answer</h2>
      <p>
        Signed builds are a planned milestone and have not shipped. If you are here to <em>use</em>
        Ephesus — not yet. Star the repository and come back.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you are here to <em>build</em> it, everything below applies and you are very welcome.
        What exists today is real and runs: the Electron shell, the terminal vertical, the 2D floor,
        real agent CLIs under management, the Agora, the Odeon, the Stoa and the Gymnasium loop.
        What it cannot yet do is survive being packaged and handed to a stranger.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Getting it running</span>
      <h2>What a fresh clone gives you</h2>
      <p>
        You need <strong>Node 20</strong>, a toolchain that can compile native modules
        (<code>node-pty</code> and <code>better-sqlite3</code> build on install), and at least one
        agent CLI on your <code>PATH</code> if you want to drive real agents. The app runs without
        one — it says it has no engine rather than pretending to work.
      </p>
      <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/Cassandra-Collective/Ephesus.git
cd Ephesus
npm install
npm run dev</code></pre>
      <p>
        Then confirm your checkout is healthy. All three must be green on a fresh clone; if they
        are not, that is a bug and we want the issue.
      </p>
      <pre><code>npm run typecheck &amp;&amp; npm run lint &amp;&amp; npm test</code></pre>
      <div class="callout">
        <h3>What will be missing, on purpose</h3>
        <p>
          The floor’s sprite sheets are commercially licensed and are <strong>not in this
          repository</strong> — only our own index files are. Without them the floor reports a
          visible missing-tileset state rather than crashing, which is the same rule the whole app
          follows: every degradation is visible, never silent. You can develop everything else
          against that.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Before you open a PR</span>
      <h2>The rules that surprise people</h2>
      <ol class="numbered">
        <li><b>The docs are the source of truth, not the code.</b> When they disagree, the code is wrong. Precedence is design document, then decision records, then specification, then README.</li>
        <li><b>Accepted decision records are append-only.</b> Never edit one — supersede it. CI enforces this on every pull request.</li>
        <li><b>The invariants are non-negotiable.</b> Violating one is a defect even when everything appears to work.</li>
        <li><b>No new dependencies without a decision memo.</b> Open an issue before you write the import.</li>
        <li><b>A wiring seam with no test is a defect, not a gap.</b> Your evidence names the production call path — file and line — or records that there is none.</li>
        <li><b>No AI attribution anywhere in the tree.</b> No agent, session or model name in commits, code, comments or docs. CI has a tripwire for it.</li>
      </ol>
      <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Definition of done</h3>
      <ul class="dod">{dod.map((d) => <li>{d}</li>)}</ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Where to start</span>
      <h2>{issues.length} issues you could pick up today</h2>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-bottom:26px">
        Every one is grounded in something checkable — the untested-module list in the coverage
        record, or a field genuinely absent from <code>package.json</code>. None of them dissolve
        when you open the file.
      </p>
      <div class="ilist">
        {issues.map((i) => (
          <a class="issue" href={`https://github.com/Cassandra-Collective/Ephesus/issues/${i.n}`}>
            <div class="inum">#{i.n}</div>
            <div>
              <h3>{i.title}<span class={i.tag === 'help wanted' ? 'tag hw' : 'tag'}>{i.tag}</span></h3>
              <p>{i.note}</p>
            </div>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p>
        This is a curated snapshot of open issues. For the latest options, see the
        <a href="https://github.com/Cassandra-Collective/Ephesus/issues?q=is%3Aissue%20is%3Aopen%20label%3A%22good%20first%20issue%22">live good first issue list</a>
        on GitHub.
      </p>
      <div class="btnrow">
        <a class="btn solid" href="https://github.com/Cassandra-Collective/Ephesus/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md">Read CONTRIBUTING</a>
        <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/Cassandra-Collective/Ephesus/issues">All issues</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    .pagehead { padding: 62px 0 44px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--paper-3); }
    .callout { border: 2px solid var(--ink); border-left-width: 6px; border-left-color: var(--gold); background: #FBF9F4; padding: 20px 22px; margin: 26px 0 0; }
    .callout h3 { margin: 0 0 8px; font-size: 16px; }
    .callout p { margin: 0; font-size: 16px; }
    .numbered { padding-left: 0; list-style: none; counter-reset: r; max-width: 76ch; }
    .numbered li { counter-increment: r; position: relative; padding: 12px 0 12px 46px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--paper-3); font-size: 16.5px; }
    .numbered li::before {
      content: counter(r); position: absolute; left: 0; top: 12px;
      font-family: var(--pixel); font-size: 11px; color: var(--terracotta);
    }
    .dod { padding-left: 0; list-style: none; max-width: 76ch; }
    .dod li { padding: 9px 0 9px 26px; position: relative; font-size: 16px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--paper-3); }
    .dod li::before { content: ""; position: absolute; left: 0; top: 18px; width: 10px; height: 10px; background: var(--olive); }
    .ilist { border: 2px solid var(--ink); background: #FBF9F4; }
    .issue { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 74px 1fr; gap: 14px; padding: 18px 22px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--paper-3); border-left: 0; border-right: 0; border-top: 0; color: var(--ink); }
    .issue:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
    .issue:hover { background: var(--paper-2); color: var(--ink); }
    .inum { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 14px; color: var(--ink-3); padding-top: 2px; }
    .issue h3 { margin: 0 0 4px; font-size: 16.5px; }
    .issue p { margin: 0; font-size: 15px; color: var(--ink-3); max-width: 70ch; }
    .tag {
      display: inline-block; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 9.5px; letter-spacing: .1em;
      text-transform: uppercase; background: var(--olive); color: #FBF9F4;
      padding: 3px 7px; margin-left: 10px; vertical-align: middle;
    }
    .tag.hw { background: var(--aegean); }
  </style>
</Base>
